title: GUI scripting endpoints
tags: [[Scripting the GUI]]
kind: reference
summary: The /gui/* routes of the GUI's server — workspaces, layout, panel views, commands, messages, progress, and the two waits that block on the user.
audience: dev


Served by the GUI's own server on `127.0.0.1`, port `gui-port` (7524;
[[GUI configuration]]), by `crates/gui/src/server/gui_api.rs`. Every `/gui/*` request
needs the GUI's session token in `Authorization: Bearer` ([[Session tokens]]):
`mf gui` sends it, a bare `curl` gets `401`. The routes answer as long as the GUI runs,
with or without the daemon; the few that ask the daemon something do it best-effort.
Errors are `{"error": "..."}`: `404` for an unknown workspace, `400` for a malformed
request.

The Rust side owns the state these routes read and write, and the shell mirrors it
through events, so a script and the window always agree ([[GUI]]).

|!Route |!Does |
|<<cmd "GET /gui/workspaces">> |lists the workspaces |
|<<cmd "POST /gui/workspaces">> |creates one |
|<<cmd "DELETE /gui/workspaces/:id">> |closes one |
|<<cmd "GET /gui/workspaces/:id/vars/:key">> |reads one workspace variable |
|<<cmd "GET /gui/layout">>, <<cmd "PUT /gui/layout">> |which workspace each slot shows |
|<<cmd "GET /gui/panels/:slot/view">>, <<cmd "PUT /gui/panels/:slot/view">> |the panel type of a slot |
|<<cmd "POST /gui/command">> |runs a command |
|<<cmd "POST /gui/message">> |posts a status message |
|<<cmd "POST /gui/progress">> |updates a script's task-bar entry |
|<<cmd "POST /gui/input">> |waits for one key |
|<<cmd "POST /gui/prompt">> |waits for a line in the command input |
|<<cmd "GET /gui/status">> |the whole state at once |
|<<cmd "GET /gui/bench">>, <<cmd "POST /gui/bench/clear">> |the recorded timings |

!! The run id

A script the GUI starts — `script:run`, a `!` line, `file:open-with` — runs in its own
process group with `METAFOLDER_GUI_TASK` set to its run id (`script-3`, counted from
the GUI's start). `mf gui` sends it as `task` on the four requests that take one. It
is what ties a request to a script: the workspaces it owns, its task-bar entry, the
process group to signal ([[Script sessions]]). An absent or unknown `task` is never an
error — the request then belongs to nobody — so the same script runs from a terminal.

!! Workspaces

`GET /gui/workspaces` answers an array:

```
[{"id": "ws-1", "name": "music 1", "active_repo": "<uuid>", "repo_name": "music"},
 {"id": "ws-2", "name": "Workspace 1", "active_repo": null, "repo_name": null}]
```

`repo_name` is the repository's name as it was when the workspace adopted it
([[Workspaces]]).

`POST /gui/workspaces` takes
`{"active_repo": "<uuid>", "task": "script-3", "panels": {"left": "file"}}`, all
optional, and answers `{"id": "ws-3"}`. Without `active_repo` the workspace gets the first repository the
daemon lists, or none. With `task` it belongs to that script. `panels` names the panel
type a slot shows the first time the workspace is put there, in place of its default
one; a slot other than `left` or `right` is a `400`.

`DELETE /gui/workspaces/:id` answers `204`. A slot that showed it shows the previous
workspace in tab order, or none when it was the last.

`GET /gui/workspaces/:id/vars/:key` answers `{"value": <json>}`. A variable never set
reads as `null`, with `200`: ''nothing published yet'' is an answer a script falls back
from, and only an unknown workspace is `404` ([[Workspace variables]]). `mf gui query`
and `mf gui selected` are built on it.

!! Layout and views

`GET /gui/layout` answers `{"left": "ws-1", "right": null}` — `null` for a hidden
slot. `PUT /gui/layout` takes the same shape and changes only the keys present; `null`
hides a slot. It answers `{}`.

`PUT /gui/panels/:slot/view` (`left` or `right`) takes

```
{"type": "file", "path": "/abs/file.jpg"}
```

and answers `{}`. A hidden slot is shown first; one with no workspace
takes the focused slot's, and `409` when there is none.

`path` counts only with `"type": "file"`, and selects that file in the slot's
workspace:

* a path sets `selected_metarecord` to the metarecord at that path — `null` for an untracked file, or when the daemon does not answer — then `selected_paths` to `[path]`. In that order, the lookup done before either write: a panel reacting to the path reads the metarecord right then ([[Cross-panel selection]]).
* no `path` leaves the selection as it is.
* an ''empty'' `path` clears both. A script previewing the entry it asks about reaches entries with no file, and leaving the previous file on screen would present it as the subject of the question.

Any other member of the body is ignored: there is no way to hand a panel an initial
state.

`GET /gui/panels/:slot/view` answers `{"type": "file", "status": "ready"}` — or
`"loading"` until the panel has mounted — and `404` for a slot showing nothing.

!! Commands

`POST /gui/command` takes `{"invocation": "panel:set type file", "timeout_ms": null}`.
The line is handed to the shell's own dispatcher — the one the command input and the
keys use, so there is a single path — and parsed like typed text; a leading `!` runs a
shell line. The request blocks until the command resolves:

|!Answer |!Means |
|`{"event": "ok"}` |it ran |
|`{"event": "error", "message": "..."}` |it failed, or is unknown |
|`{"event": "timeout"}` |`timeout_ms` passed first |
|`{"event": "closed"}` |the GUI is closing |

All with `200`; an empty or missing `invocation` is `400`. Several may be in flight at
once. ''Resolved'' means the command and what it awaits returned — not that a panel it
opened has finished drawing.

!! Messages and progress

`POST /gui/message` takes `{"text": "...", "timeout_ms": null}` and posts it on the
status bar and in the message log of the workspace named by `?workspace_id=`, by
default the focused one (`409` when the focused slot has no workspace). `timeout_ms` is accepted
and hides nothing any more ([[Status bar and task bar]]).

`POST /gui/progress` takes

```
{"task": "script-3", "done": 12, "total": 30, "phase": "/music/x.mp3"}
```

every member optional, and overwrites only what is given. It always answers `{}`.

!! The two waits

`POST /gui/input` and `POST /gui/prompt` block until the user answers. They share
''one lock'': while either waits, a second call of either is refused with `409`, not
queued ([[GUI design decisions]]).

`POST /gui/input` takes

```
{"keys": ["left", "right", "q"], "prompt": "Which one?", "task": "script-3", "timeout_ms": null}
```

every member optional. `prompt` is the question shown in its bar (''Waiting for input''
without one). A key the GUI reserves makes the call fail with `400` ''before'' the lock
is taken, so the next script is free to ask. With no `keys`, only `answer:send`
answers.

While it waits, each key is bound to `answer:send <key>` in the compiled keybinding
table, and the shell looks at the awaited keys ''before'' the matcher, so no narrower
binding wins. Unticking the script keys removes those bindings — a disabled key must
not stay bound as a fallback. The shell learns all of it from the `input-wait-changed`
event: `active`, `temp_keys`, `prompt`, `workspaces` (the asking script's, empty when
nobody's), `task` and `script_keys`; it is sent again, with the table, when the
checkbox flips.

|!Answer |!Means |
|`{"event": "answer", "value": "left"}` |a key, or `answer:send`; `back` for Backspace |
|`{"event": "timeout"}` |`timeout_ms` passed |
|`{"event": "closed"}` |the GUI is closing, or `script:stop` closed a question nobody owns |

`POST /gui/prompt` takes

```
{"prompt": "Rating (1-5): ", "completions": ["1", "2"], "task": "script-3", "timeout_ms": null}
```

of which only `prompt` is required. `completions` are
offered by the command input, ranked and navigated like command names; any text may
still be typed.

|!Answer |!Means |
|`{"event": "confirm", "text": "4"}` |Enter |
|`{"event": "cancel"}` |Escape |
|`{"event": "timeout"}`, `{"event": "closed"}` |as above |

The lock is released by a guard dropped on ''every'' way out — an answer, a timeout,
and the request going away. A script killed while it waits drops its connection, and
the handler's future with it: released only after the `await`, the lock stayed held
for the rest of the GUI's life, and the next script's first question got `409` with
nothing on screen to say why.

!! Status

```
{"workspaces": [...],
 "layout": {"left":  {"workspace_id": "ws-1", "panel_type": "metarecord-list", "focused": true},
            "right": null},
 "daemon_connected": true,
 "input_wait_active": false}
```

`workspaces` is the array of `GET /gui/workspaces`; a hidden slot is `null`.

!! Bench

`GET /gui/bench` answers `{"records": [{"name": "mf:list:render", "duration_ms": 2.0}]}`:
the phases the panels timed and every daemon call the shell made for them,
kept in memory since the last `POST /gui/bench/clear`. A driver clears, runs a
scenario through `POST /gui/command`, and reads the timings back ([[Benchmarks]]).
